The sequence $x_n$ converges to $c$ in $\ell_1$, meaning $\|x_n - c\|_1 \to 0$. For any $p > 1$, the $\ell_p$ norm of $x_n - c$ satisfies $\|x_n - c\|_p \leq \|x_n - c\|_1$ due to the inequality between $\ell_p$ and $\ell_1$ norms (since $|x_n(k) - c(k)|^p \leq |x_n(k) - c(k)|$ for sufficiently large $k$ where the terms are small). Thus, $\|x_n - c\|_p \to 0$, implying convergence in $\ell_p$.

**Final Answer**
\boxed{Yes}

