The matrix $ Q $ has orthonormal columns, meaning $ Q^T Q = I_{n \times n} $. However, $ QQ^T $ results in an $ m \times m $ matrix. Since $ m > n $, the rank of $ Q $ is $ n $, making $ QQ^T $ a projection matrix onto the $ n $-dimensional column space of $ Q $, not the full $ m $-dimensional identity matrix. Thus, $ QQ^T \neq I_{m \times m} $.

**Final Answer**
\boxed{False}

